From bc6fbd9a894e092d1c57811e6feb8b89b061ebc1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nekokabu <nekokabu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 08:26:33 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] n64: CPU write buffer, FPU operand costs and cache-op
 timing from hardware (cpu_timing v18, rdp_timing v17)

cpu_timing v18 measured what v14 could not separate:

- FPU: the costs v11/v14 saw (add/sub/mul issuing in 2 cycles, div.d 30) were the
  hardware's fast paths for trivial operands (adding 0.0, multiplying by 0.0, 1.0 or a
  power of two, dividing 0.0). With ordinary operands add/sub take 3 cycles, mul.s 5,
  mul.d 8, div.s and sqrt.s 29, div.d and sqrt.d 58, conversions 5, compares 1, and a
  dependent instruction waits one more cycle. Use those; the fast paths are not modelled.
- Write buffer: stores that leave the CPU (uncached RDRAM, RCP registers, dirty lines) go
  through a 4-entry buffer and drain in order, an uncached word occupying the bus for 16
  cycles, an SP/DP register write 10, an MI register write 2. The CPU only waits when the
  buffer is full, when a line fill or an uncached read needs the bus (a fill also pays the
  9-cycle bus turnaround after a write: +25 behind one posted word), or when a store
  follows a dirty-line write-back that has not left yet. A dirty victim's write-back is
  mostly hidden behind the following instructions (+6 when the next miss is 32 cycles
  away, +28 when it follows at once); it was charged as 25 serial cycles.
- A cache operation costs 5 cycles; the line it writes back leaves through the buffer.

rdp_timing v17 replayed the game display lists beside combined CPU/RSP loads: the losses
add up (no super-additive term), a CPU write-back costs the RDP about 1 clock instead of
5, and each uncached CPU write costs it about 4 (2.7 with the VI off, 5 in the games' VI
mode), which was not modelled.

